OLD:
  \caption{Model-state offloading's premise under LoRA. (a) The
  optimizer step is a few percent of step time for dense (Qwen3-32B)
  and MoE (Qwen3-30B-A3B) LoRA fine-tuning, so runtime machinery
  centered on it moves little. (b) With model states off the GPU,
  activations dominate the remaining memory peak at long context.}
NEW:
  \caption{Model-state offloading's premise under LoRA. (a) The
  optimizer step is at most a tenth of a percent of step time for
  Qwen3-30B-A3B and Mixtral-8x22B LoRA fine-tuning at 128K tokens
  (batch 1) under both ZeRO-3 Offload and SuperOffload (the
  optimizer band is drawn at a minimum visible height; labels give
  the measured share), and each model's two steps are near-identical
  ($0.99\times$ both) --- runtime machinery centered on the
  optimizer moves nothing end-to-end. (b) Peak training memory under
  SuperOffload (rank-16 adapters, batch 1) at a shorter and a longer
  sequence length: the offloaded model states (hatched: frozen
  weights and adapter/optimizer state, CPU-resident) are constant in
  sequence length, while the GPU-resident activation footprint (saved
  activations and transient working set) grows with context ---
  Qwen3-30B-A3B 55$\to$177~GiB from 192K to 640K tokens,
  Mixtral-8x22B 85$\to$161~GiB from 128K to 256K --- until the GPU
  share alone overflows the device (OOM marks: measured capacity
  walls at 800K and 320K): at long context, activations are the
  memory problem offloading does not solve.}
# (CPU segments are arithmetic estimates from the runs' exact trainable
#  counts — 843.8M / 519.3M params, fp32 W+G+m+v — + frozen bf16 bytes;
#  GPU segments are measured allocator-peak breakdowns.)

